{#- alloy (H5.8, docs/target-architecture/host/h5-pi-compose.md). Grafana Alloy on daniel-pi, shipping
    this host's container logs and its two token heartbeats' verdict lines to loki-homelab. It
    was an Ansible role's compose file until H5.8. The macros that file imported (networks,
    resources, autokuma labels) are written out here, because a service tree has no
    ansible/templates on its search path. The values are the ones the macros rendered for the Pi.
    This header holds what the role's CLAUDE.md said; the comments below carry the rest.

    - **Pin policy.** The image is pinned by tag AND digest, moved by hand when the cluster's
      Alloy (`roles/k8s/loki-homelab/defaults/main.yml`) moves. H5.11 adds a Renovate manager
      for this file.
    - **Host.** daniel-pi only. The cluster nodes run the loki-homelab DaemonSet.
    - **The port is on the Pi's LAN IP.** Prometheus scrapes it as `alloy-pi`, and everything
      but `/metrics` needs basic auth (the `DECIDED:` marker on `ports:` below).
    - **Sources.** Container logs through docker-proxy (`job="pi"`), and
      `/var/log/pi-health/*.log` (`job="syslog"`, `machine="daniel-pi"`). The label contract is
      guarded by hosts/lib/tests/test_pi_compose_alloy.py.
    - **One source is counted, not shipped.** `/var/log/pi-health/crowdsec-edge-probe.status`
      holds the edge probe's once-a-minute HTTP status for crowdsec's off-cluster verify. A
      `stage.metrics` counter in config.alloy turns it into
      `loki_process_custom_crowdsec_edge_probe_total` by `status`, which Prometheus reads
      through the `alloy-pi` scrape, and a drop stage discards every line. The verify's ban
      makes Traefik refuse this container's Loki pushes, so the count must not depend on them.
      The timer that writes the file is `hosts/components/crowdsec-probe/`, and the same test
      pins the stage. The label values render from `loki_streams` in `group_vars/all.yml`,
      which the cluster's Alloy and monitor-bridge's selectors also read;
      `ansible/tests/services/test_loki_stream_labels.py` holds the consumers to it.
    - **Config.** config.alloy (River) is a plain package file beside this one, mounted
      read-only. It carries no secret: it reads the HTTP password as
      `sys.env("ALLOY_PI_HTTP_PASSWORD")` and the domain as `sys.env("HOMELAB_DOMAIN")`. The
      password reaches the environment below through the project's Compose env file
      (`alloy_pi_http_password`; the operator runs `host_secrets.py sync daniel-pi` after a
      rotation). The domain is a plain inventory variable, rendered at build time. A secret
      rotation changes this environment, so `up -d` recreates the container. A config edit
      changes the `homelab.config-sha256` label, which replaces the role's
      `common_config_changed` flag: Alloy reads its config once, at startup, so without the
      label an edit would sit inert while the deploy reported green.
    - **The host journal is not shipped, and RSS is the reason (#1922, measured 2026-09-17).**
      `loki.source.journal` works on this image — the 2026-09-02 spike read 6,573 lines in ten
      minutes — and a `docker.service` unit matcher would ship ~50 lines a day (54 lines for
      the four days to 2026-09-17), so line volume is not the cost. The container's memory is.
      `process_resident_memory_bytes{job="alloy-pi"}` over the 7 days to 2026-09-17: median
      59.3 MB, p95 70.1 MB, and a daily maximum of 75.5–82.1 MB on every one of the seven
      days, against the 96 MiB (100.7 MB) `resources()` cap and a 72 MiB GOMEMLIMIT.
      `go_memstats_sys_bytes` read 96.3 MB at the same time. #1944 settled what that peak is
      (2026-09-18): the top of the GC sawtooth, sampled 1,440 times a day. The live heap is
      38 MB, not the 19 MiB the sizing assumed, so GOGC=50 set a 58 MB heap goal and the Go
      runtime's total reached the 72 MiB GOMEMLIMIT at the top of every cycle; since #1967
      (2026-09-18) GOGC is off and a 104 MiB limit under a 128M cap is the only collection
      trigger, so the peak is ~20 MB higher and collection runs a third as often (0.18 cycles
      a minute) — the Go runtime's forced two-minute cycle, which no GOGC value escapes, is
      what GOGC=off removes. That cut the container's major faults 24% (72/s from 95/s); the
      rest are its 60-second work re-faulting a cold working set, which no GC setting reaches.
      Nothing scheduled drives it, and there is no removable cause: what fills the 38 MB is
      unmeasured and pprof is off by decision. The GOMEMLIMIT and `resources` comments below
      carry the numbers, and hosts/lib/tests/test_pi_compose_alloy.py takes its floor from that
      live heap. The journal reader's own footprint is still unmeasured. It is also awkward to
      measure: sdjournal mmaps the journal files, and here those sit on the 128 MB log2ram
      tmpfs, so the mapped window counts in this process's RSS (the metric above) while the
      physical pages stay journald's — the metric and the cgroup cap would disagree, and only a
      deployed before/after on this host settles it. A reader whose cost cannot be measured
      without a deploy, added to a process whose runtime already paces against its memory
      limit, is the case the issue's own drop rule names. The daemon-failure evidence the
      journal would have carried reaches Loki another way: when dockerd stops answering,
      `pi-recovery-health` (`hosts/components/pi-heartbeats/`) appends the newest non-info
      `journalctl -u docker` lines to its own DOWN record, which the `pi_health` source in
      config.alloy already ships. To revisit the reader, first make room for it — a lower
      `GOGC` takes ~10 MB off every peak per halving, and the `DECIDED:` marker at the `GOGC`
      line says what that costs on this host (the heap is faulted back from zram once per
      cycle, so every halving of GOGC doubles the faults) — then measure RSS for a week and
      delete `test_the_journal_is_not_shipped` in the same PR. The test is the enforcement for
      this decision.
    - **No healthcheck, on purpose.** The image ships no HTTP client, so a `wget` probe fails
      to EXEC and reads as `unhealthy`; Prometheus `up{job="alloy-pi"}` covers liveness from
      outside. The comment above `ports:` carries the history.
    - **Runs as 1000:1000 with `--storage.path=/data`**, not the image's uid 473 or its
      `/var/lib/alloy`: the comments at `command:` and `user:` say why each half is
      load-bearing.
    - **`depends_on: docker-proxy`** replaces the Ansible play's list-order rule. Discovery goes
      through that read-only proxy, which the docker-proxy service file in this project
      declares. The Watchtower `depends-on` label is not carried over: Watchtower was retired
      (35c0dc8d) and nothing reads the label. -#}
---

services:
  alloy:
    container_name: alloy
    # Tag AND digest, unlike the :latest@sha256 the Promtail role carried: Alloy is the shipper
    # the cluster pins by tag in roles/k8s/loki-homelab/defaults/main.yml, and keeping the two
    # on one version keeps one label contract. Renovate does not track hosts/components/pi-compose/** until H5.11 —
    # no manager's matchFileNames reaches it (renovate.json) — so this pin moves by hand when
    # the cluster's does. The digest is the arm64 manifest, resolved on daniel-pi 2026-09-02.
    image: grafana/alloy:v1.19.2@sha256:b8ec653c44235fbe910879145dac3597d66b0aaecf60bcbbe82580767771a839
    restart: unless-stopped
    command:
      - run
      - /etc/alloy/config.alloy
      # Alloy binds 127.0.0.1 by default; Prometheus scrapes this from the LAN.
      - --server.http.listen-addr=0.0.0.0:12345
      # The tailing cursor lives here — see the data volume below. NOT the image's own
      # /var/lib/alloy/data: that parent is 0770 uid 473 in the image, so uid 1000 cannot
      # traverse it to reach a bind mount inside, and Alloy dies at startup with
      # `mkdir /var/lib/alloy/data: permission denied` — every two minutes, on the first
      # deploy of this role (2026-09-02). Root never hits it, which is why the cluster
      # DaemonSet and the spike that sized this container both ran.
      - --storage.path=/data
      # Same reason Loki's own config sets analytics.reporting_enabled false: Pi-hole blocks
      # stats.grafana.org and each report would spend its retry budget on a name that never
      # resolves.
      - --disable-reporting
      # /debug/pprof and /-/support answer by default in this build (v1.19.2's
      # internal/alloycli/cmd_run.go: enablePprof true, disableSupportBundle false) and the
      # port below is published on the LAN. pprof hands any LAN client a profiler that costs
      # this 456 MB Zero 2 W real CPU, and /-/support returns a bundle carrying the running
      # config. Neither has a consumer here. See the DECIDED marker on `ports:` for the rest
      # of the surface and why the port itself stays published (issue #1130).
      - --server.http.enable-pprof=false
      - --server.http.disable-support-bundle
    # The image's own uid is 473, and setup_dirs.yml creates the bind-mount source owned by
    # the deploy user (1000). Left at the default, Alloy cannot write its positions under
    # --storage.path. With no positions to resume from, every restart re-reads every
    # container's log from the beginning and re-ships it. Matches the estate's PUID/PGID.
    # The flip side is the storage path above: as 1000 this process cannot enter the
    # image's /var/lib/alloy at all.
    user: "1000:1000"
    cap_drop:
      - ALL
    security_opt:
      - no-new-privileges:true
    read_only: true
    tmpfs:
      - /tmp
    networks:
{% for net in container_item.networks %}
      - {{ net }}
{% endfor %}
    depends_on:
      - docker-proxy
    # Docker copies the HOST's resolver options into every container's resolv.conf, and this
    # host's are `timeout:2 attempts:1` (optimize_pi_dns_options) — sized so a dead Pi-hole
    # costs one 2s wait before 1.1.1.1 answers. Inside a container that same single 2s try
    # also governs the hop to Docker's embedded DNS at 127.0.0.11, which is dockerd itself,
    # and dockerd stalls for seconds on this Zero 2 W whenever it is busy with a container or
    # network operation. Every 60s pass here opens a fresh connection per container, so one
    # such stall fails six lookups at once — `lookup docker-proxy on 127.0.0.11:53: i/o
    # timeout`, at level=error, six in one 350ms burst on 2026-09-03 eight seconds before a
    # `kill promtail` event, against 0ms for the same lookup at idle (issue #927). Neither
    # side is broken: the resolver is idle-fast, and CFS throttling at the 0.30 cap can hold
    # this process at most 70ms per period, so it cannot manufacture a 2s timeout. Three 3s
    # tries ride out the stall. `attempts` is rounds over the nameserver list, and the list
    # here is one entry, so this is three tries against dockerd — the upstream failover the
    # host's values were tuned for happens inside dockerd's own forwarder, not on this hop.
    dns_opt:
      - timeout:3
      - attempts:3
    volumes:
      # The config is a package file (a plain file the renderer installs beside this one), not a
      # bind-mounted file the Ansible role rendered. It is mode 0644, not the role's 0640:
      # it carries no secret now.
      - type: bind
        source: /usr/lib/homelab/compose/alloy/config.alloy
        target: /etc/alloy/config.alloy
        read_only: true
        bind: {create_host_path: false}
      # Positions must survive a restart. Without this the container re-reads every
      # container's log from the beginning on each recreate, which both duplicates lines in
      # Loki and spends the Pi's uplink re-sending them.
      - type: bind
        source: ./data
        target: /data
        bind: {create_host_path: false}
      # The two host health timers' verdict lines (pi_health source). Read-only, and the same
      # path inside and out so the config's path needs no translation. No group_add: the
      # timers' services write as uid 1000 and this container already runs as 1000:1000.
      - type: bind
        source: /var/log/pi-health
        target: /var/log/pi-health
        read_only: true
        bind: {create_host_path: false}
    environment:
      # The two values config.alloy reads. The password is a SOPS key that the project's Compose
      # env file fills; the domain is a plain variable, rendered at build time.
      - ALLOY_PI_HTTP_PASSWORD={{ alloy_pi_http_password }}
      - HOMELAB_DOMAIN={{ domain }}
      # A soft ceiling on the Go runtime's TOTAL memory (heap, stacks, GC metadata, everything
      # `go_memstats_sys_bytes` counts), not on the live heap. When the total reaches it the
      # runtime collects continuously, and the only thing bounding that is the GC CPU limiter
      # at 50% of GOMAXPROCS. This shipped as 48MiB on 2026-09-02, sized from RSS (34-43 MiB)
      # rather than from the runtime total, and that is exactly the state it ran in: sys_bytes
      # 58.7 MB against a 48 MiB limit, 2 GC cycles per second to ship 1.6 log lines per
      # second, and 0.27-0.29 of a core steady for 7 days — the limiter's 50% of one processor,
      # and the largest single load on the host (#932, the host contention behind #930).
      #
      # Sized 2026-09-03 as ~20 MiB over a 58.7 MB `go_memstats_sys_bytes`. The live heap it
      # was sized against was a first-day reading: the post-GC floor of
      # `go_memstats_heap_alloc_bytes` climbed 29 → 38.5 MB over the three days after that
      # restart and has held at 38.3-38.5 MB every day since (measured 2026-09-18 over 16 days;
      # a fresh process starts at 22 MB, and the cluster's Alloy DaemonSet floors at 27 MB).
      # That is warm-up to a plateau, not a leak, and what is in the 38 MB is unmeasured —
      # pprof is off by decision (#1130). With GOGC=off below, this limit is what starts a
      # collection, and the distance from the live set plus the runtime's non-heap classes
      # (38 + 8 MB) up to it is one cycle's worth of allocation — ~50 MB, ~350 s at the
      # measured 145 KB/s. The 72 MiB limit under GOGC=50 was reached at the top of every
      # cycle too (#1944): what a limit that is reached does is force the scavenger to hand
      # idle heap pages back to the kernel, which is cheap. The continuous-collection state
      # above needs the limit under the live set plus GC slack, which is why the guard in
      # hosts/lib/tests/test_pi_compose_alloy.py takes the floor from the
      # measured live heap, not from `sys_bytes` — that metric never shrinks and read 96.3 MB
      # on 2026-09-17, above the old limit and the old cap alike. With GOGC off the guard
      # requires at least the slack GOGC=50 gave, half the live heap, so a "thrifty" edit
      # here cannot shorten the cycle below the 2026-09-03 shape.
      - GOMEMLIMIT=104MiB
      # GOGC=off: the memory limit above is the only thing that starts a collection. CPU is
      # not cheaper than RAM on this host after all: the 1c9592c1 shape spent 0.28 cores to
      # save ~10 MiB, and GOGC=50 (2026-09-03 to 2026-09-18) ran a 58 MB goal at 0.52 cycles
      # a minute.
      #
      # DECIDED: GOGC=off under GOMEMLIMIT, to cut how often this heap is faulted back in,
      # and the faults that remain are accepted (#1967). This host swaps by design
      # (`vm.swappiness=130` to zram, roles/setup/optimize_pi), global reclaim swaps this
      # heap out between cycles, and the mark phase faults it back in. Measured 2026-09-18 at
      # GOGC=50: 28,423 major faults on this cgroup in 300 s (95/s), a third of the host's
      # 84,938, 0.52 cycles a minute — 10,800 faults a cycle against a 9,500-page heap, so by
      # the next cycle the whole heap is cold. GOGC=25 the same day, after a 16-minute
      # warm-up: 1.0 cycles a minute, 33,667 in 300 s of the host's 100,479 — the same 33.5%
      # share, at the same host pressure (memory PSI `full avg300` 4.0-4.6%), so no
      # reduction; 6,700 faults a cycle, half the heap. The rate is cycles × the part of the
      # heap that went cold, and below one cycle every ~2 minutes that part is all of it, so
      # fewer cycles means proportionally fewer faults.
      #
      # GOGC alone cannot get there. The Go runtime's sysmon forces a cycle whenever two
      # minutes pass without one (`forcegcperiod`), so at this allocation rate (145 KB/s)
      # every GOGC from 50 up sits on that floor: GOGC=100 measured 0.44 cycles a minute
      # under an 88 MiB limit and 0.43 under 104 MiB, against the 0.23 its 78 MB goal
      # implies, and cut the faults 20% (24,410 in a clean 300 s window, 81/s, 26.9% of the
      # host's 90,912) rather than the 50% halving the cycles would. The time trigger is
      # skipped when GOGC is off (`gcTrigger.test` returns false for `gcTriggerTime` at
      # `gcPercent < 0`), and then the limit is the trigger: the ~50 MB between the live set
      # plus runtime (~47 MB) and 104 MiB is one cycle's allocation. Measured 2026-09-18 in
      # a clean 600 s window 23 minutes after the start: 0.18 cycles a minute (one per
      # ~330 s, runtime peak 97 MiB), 43,288 faults (72/s), 26.9% of the host's 161,051 — a
      # 24% cut against the 95/s and 33.5% of GOGC=50, not the two-thirds the cycle count
      # alone predicts. Per cycle that is ~23,800 faults, over twice the heap, so most of
      # this process's faults are not the mark at all: they are its 60-second work (the
      # discovery pass, the scrape, the batch push, the tail) re-faulting a working set that
      # goes cold between rounds, and no GC setting reaches those. The heap above the live
      # set is exactly the part that sits cold, so it costs RAM as compressed swap only:
      # `memory.swap.current` read 60-71 MB in that window against 25-56 MB before, ~3 MB at
      # zram's measured 4.61:1. Pinning the heap instead (`memswap_limit` equal to the cap)
      # would hold 40-60 MB resident for the ~12 MB zram keeps it in, on a host with
      # 10-25 MB free. Judged the day after by
      # `rate(node_vmstat_pswpin{job="node-pi"}[1d])` against the 136-181/s of 2026-09-03 →
      # 09-17; the Pi memory budget review of 2026-09-18
      # (~/.claude/artifacts/pi-memory-budget-review_2026-09-18.md) has the whole host's
      # numbers.
      - GOGC=off
      # One GC-visible processor. Alloy defaults GOMAXPROCS to the 4 cores on this Pi and
      # spawns a worker set to match, which is pure contention for a job this small on a box
      # that already showed RCU stalls from fork rate.
      - GOMAXPROCS=1
    # NO healthcheck, and this is a correction rather than an omission — inherited from the
    # Promtail role, where a wget probe of /ready shipped and could never work: that image
    # carried no wget, curl, nc or busybox, so every probe exited -1 with "executable file
    # not found in PATH", the container went `unhealthy` after its third failure, and autoheal
    # restarted it on a loop. Alloy's image (Ubuntu-based, the alloy binary and libc) has no
    # HTTP client either. The general rule stands: on this host a healthcheck is only
    # available to an image that ships an HTTP client, and `test:` failing to EXEC is
    # indistinguishable from the service being down.
    #
    # Liveness is covered from outside instead, and better: Prometheus scrapes 12345 as
    # `alloy-pi`, so a dead shipper is `up == 0` on a target that check_cluster_targets
    # already watches. That check sees a wedged shipper this probe could not have.
    # DECIDED: the port stays published on the LAN and the surface behind it is narrowed
    # instead. Two consumers reach it from off-box and neither can use a loopback or a bridge
    # address: observability's `alloy-pi` Prometheus job scrapes k8s_pi_client_ip:12345 as a
    # STATIC target (roles/k8s/observability/templates/prometheus.yaml.j2), and monitor-bridge's
    # detached-container arm expects `alloy` to report a `->` mapping (PI_PUBLISHED_PORTS,
    # pinned by ansible/tests/services/test_pi_publishing_containers.py). Metrics are why the
    # port exists: dropped entries, batch retries and send latency say whether shipping is
    # keeping up, and a log shipper that silently stops is the failure this makes visible.
    #
    # Everything else that answered here is closed rather than published. Alloy v1.19.2 serves
    # /metrics, /-/healthy, /-/ready, /-/reload (GET *and* POST, and no flag disables it),
    # /-/support, /debug/pprof and the config-graph UI on this one listener
    # (internal/service/http/http.go at that tag). The two flags above drop pprof and the
    # support bundle; config.alloy.j2's `http.auth` block requires basic auth on every path
    # EXCEPT /metrics, so the reload endpoint, the UI and /api/v0/component/ now need a
    # credential no LAN client has. Prometheus keeps scraping /metrics unauthenticated
    # exactly as before (issue #1130).
    ports:
      - "{{ server_ip }}:12345:12345/tcp"
    labels:
      # Docker-type Kuma monitors are retired fleet-wide (kuma_docker_monitors: false), so the
      # autokuma macro emitted this inert label.
      - "org.homelab.kuma-docker-monitor=retired-slice7-phase-d"
      # The sha256 of the rendered config.alloy. Compose cannot see the file's content, so the
      # label is what makes `up -d` recreate the container when the config changes.
      - "homelab.config-sha256={{ alloy_config_sha256 }}"
    # 128M is the containment cap, 24 MiB over the 104 MiB GOMEMLIMIT above so a spike is
    # collected rather than OOM-killed (96M was 24 MiB over the 72 MiB limit it paired with).
    # Startup under the 0.30 CPU cap takes minutes on this host (the binary is large and
    # arm64 decompression is slow); it is not a hang.
    #
    # `process_resident_memory_bytes{job="alloy-pi"}` peaked at 75-82 MB on every day under
    # GOGC=50, and that is the top of the GC sawtooth, not an event: the 58 MB heap goal plus 8 MB of runtime
    # plus up to 23 MB of the binary's own text pages (`RssFile`, file-backed) read at the
    # moment of a scrape. Measured 2026-09-18 over 7 days at the 60s scrape: the samples above
    # 70 MB fall in every one of the 24 hours (10-39 per hour), in 435 runs of median length
    # one sample and at most three; the container-log rate is 1.6-1.7 lines/s in every hour;
    # and no cron or timer on the host fires where the daily maxima fall (#1944). The reading
    # is noisy the other way too — 22 MB one scrape and 70 MB the next — because this host
    # swaps by design (`vm.swappiness=130` to zram, roles/setup/optimize_pi) and every GC
    # cycle faults the swapped heap back in: 66-127 major faults/s, a third of the host's.
    # Do not size from RSS or `max_over_time` of it; the cgroup is what the cap governs.
    #
    # The cgroup reached this cap 1,846 times in the first half hour after the 2026-09-18
    # 00:39Z start — the binary's pages being charged and reclaimed as it was read — and not
    # once in the 40 minutes after; `oom_kill` 0. Anonymous memory peaks around 66 MB, file
    # pages are reclaimable, and Docker's default `memory.swap.max` equals the cap, so an
    # OOM kill needs anon plus swap past twice the cap. "~20 MB under its OOM line" was the
    # RSS reading against the cap, and the two are not the same quantity.
    # DECIDED: the cap is 128M because GOGC=100 above asks for it — the 78 MB heap goal plus
    # 8 MB of runtime plus the binary's text pages clears 96M — and for no other reason. The
    # 2026-09-18 marker here read "raising it buys nothing the process asks for and costs a
    # 456 MB host that already holds 320 MB in swap"; that held for the GOGC=50 pairing and
    # the cost side still does, which is why the extra is taken as compressed swap (see the
    # GOGC marker) rather than resident pages. The cap governs the cgroup; it reserves nothing.
    deploy:
      resources:
        limits:
          cpus: '0.30'
          memory: 128M
        reservations:
          cpus: '0.02'
          memory: 24M

networks:
{% for net in container_item.networks %}
  {{ net }}:
    external: true
{% endfor %}
